raw:
{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "Are the characteristics that ordinarily help Americans engage in politics also associated with expressing political hostility on X/Twitter, alongside the factors emphasized by grievance and personality accounts?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We extend this revisionist\nperspective to online political hostility by asking whether hostile political expression\non X/Twitter is associated not only with deprivation or personality-based explanations, but also with political engagement and its antecedents.",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Are the links between engagement and hostile expression stronger among users whose feelings toward political leaders are more polarized?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Expectation 2 concerns whether the associations between engagement and hostility are stronger among users with higher\nleader-directed affective polarization.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Do users who express more political hostility post more often and have greater potential reach on the platform?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Expectation 3. Online political hostility is associated with greater activity and potential reach on X/Twitter, measured by\nposting frequency and numbers of friends and followers.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Political hostility on American X/Twitter is not solely a matter of marginalization or personality: it is positively associated with forms of political engagement that are usually valued as part of democratic citizenship.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "we\nfind that online political hostility is positively associated with several indicators of political engagement and its antecedents.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "These\nfindings suggest that online political hostility is not only associated with deprivation, marginalization, or personality-based\nexplanations, as highlighted in prior work [14,15]. It is also associated with forms of engagement that are often considered\ncentral to democratic citizenship.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The study links a six-wave U.S. YouGov panel to consenting participants’ public X/Twitter activity. Recruitment began in January 2019, the panel was expanded in September 2020, and the last survey was in January 2021. There were 2,567 linked panelists and up to 3,643 participants in analyses using only X data; survey items and participation varied by wave.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "All participants were recruited through YouGov’s standing web panel in the United States.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "We recruited the original set of panelists in January 2019.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "Participants were specifically asked if they wished to share their X handle\nto link their publicly available posts to their survey responses.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "These\nparticipants, along with an additional set of panelists who were included to increase the study’s power in September 2020,\nwere then asked to participate in five additional survey waves, administered at various time points until the final survey in\nJanuary 2021.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "The total number of panelists where we were able to link survey responses to their X handle in this period\nwas 2567. For the analyses where we only use X data the maximum number of respondents is 3643.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "The six surveys did not include the same questions,\nand, consequently, not all measures described below are available in each wave. Further, as participants were free to opt\nin and out of the different surveys, the final sample size for each wave differs.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The behavioral corpus consists of 9,250,180 posts collected from March 4, 2019, to January 2021. The unit of analysis is the individual panelist, not the post.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The scraping of X commenced on March 4, 2019 and stopped in January 2021. During this period, we managed to\nscrape 9,250,180 posts from our panelists. All measures based on X data are aggregated at the level of the individual\npanelist since our unit of analysis is individual panelists and not posts.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The primary outcome uses the Super-Unsupervised embedding method: word2vec vectors learned from panelists’ posts combine the terms ‘political’ and ‘hate’; posts are scored by cosine similarity to the resulting vector, and scores are averaged by person and, in panel models, by period.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Our key measure of online political hostility is based on a word\nembeddings approach termed the Super-Unsupervised (SU) approach [60].",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "First, we create word vectors based on posts from our panelists using the word2vec method [61,62].",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "Second, we combine the word vectors\n“political” and “hate” into a combined vector that captures the co-occurrence of political and hostile language in the corpus.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "Third, we use this combined measure to calculate the cosine similarity between individual posts and the “political hate”\nword vector",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "Fourth and finally, we create a\nmeasure of a respondent’s general level of political hostility by averaging the hostility scores of all their posts; for the panel\nmodels we compute this average per time unit investigated (wave or month, depending on the analysis).",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors summarize prior validation against 2,469 posts labeled by four experts. The dichotomized score achieved 74% accuracy on held-out posts against a roughly 50% baseline.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The SU approach was also validated against 2,469 hand-labeled posts rated by four expert raters",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "We trained the score on a training split of these labelled posts and evaluated it\non a held-out test split from the same domain (i.e., panelist posts), thereby avoiding in-sample inflation.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "Here, it reached 74% accuracy against a 50% baseline when dichotomized on a held-out test set.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The validation also checks whether the score captures political content rather than profanity alone, whether it transfers to an external embedding space, and whether high-scoring posts and aggregate trends reflect expected partisan and election patterns.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "They found that posts with high Perspective API scores\nconcentrate on profanity and insults, whereas high political hostility scores combine political referents with hostility (e.g.,\nelites/parties plus derogation), matching the construct.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "After aligning with public TwitterGloVe embeddings,\nSU “political hate” scores correlate r = .83 with scores computed in this external space",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "Dependency parsing of the\ntop 100k hostile posts shows the out-party as the grammatical actor in the large majority of cases",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "And in Denmark, Sweden, Germany, and Italy, national Political Hostility aggregates rise toward election day and peak\nexactly on that day, then decline, as expected if the measure tracks political conflict salience.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Alternative outcome measures include AFINN sentiment, Google Perspective API toxicity, the separate political and hate components of the embedding score, and a supervised classifier trained on hand-labeled posts.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the two secondary robustness measures, AFINN sentiment scores and Perspective API toxicity scores.",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "Fig 2 provides a replication of the analyses above where the composite dependent variable of online political hostility is\nsubdivided into the two components of “political” and “hate” (i.e., general hostility). In addition, as described above, we\nalso use an alternative supervised classifier of online political hostility based on a sample of hand labelled posts.",
            "page": 12
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The two distal antecedents are formal education, measured in six categories, and internal political efficacy, measured with two Center for Political Studies items and one European Social Survey item.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Internal efficacy is measured using the standard Center for Political Studies (CPS) questions [46]",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "We also use a third item from the European Social\nSurvey that asks whether “I often think it is difficult to make up my mind about political issues.”",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "Education is measured using six different educational categories: No HS, High school graduate, Some college, 2-year,\n4-year, Post-grad.",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Political interest and knowledge are measured separately using ANES items: a five-point self-rating of interest and a count of correct answers to five factual questions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Political interest is measured using a standard American National Election Studies (ANES) question asking respondents\nabout their level of political interest on a five-point scale ranging from “Extremely interested” to “Not interested at all.”",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "Political knowledge is measured using five factual questions from the ANES, scored as the number of correct\nanswers.",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The survey measures participation with separate self-reported frequency batteries covering eleven offline activities and ten online activities on the same response scale.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "To measure traditional offline participation, we created an 11-item battery\nbased on previous research [70,71], asking participants on scales ranging from “1 = Never” to “7 = Several times a day”\nhow often they participate in eleven offline political activities",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "To measure online participation, we asked participants on the same response scale how often\nthey engaged in ten online political activities [72,73]",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The six engagement indicators are analyzed separately rather than collapsed into one scale; the appendix factor analysis does not support a single dimension.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This decision to treat the indicators separately is also consistent with the analyses in\nS1 Appendix where a factor analysis does not support a one-factor measure of political engagement.",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Leader-directed affective polarization is the difference between mean ratings of in-party and out-party leaders, based on 0–100 feeling-thermometer ratings of three Republican and three Democratic politicians.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Respondents rated three Republican politicians (Ted Cruz, Donald Trump,\nand Mike Pence) and three Democratic politicians (Joe Biden, Elizabeth Warren, and Bernie Sanders) on a 0–100 feeling\nthermometer.",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "We used respondents’ party identification to distinguish in-party from out-party leaders and calculated the\ndifference between average in-party and out-party leader ratings.",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The main association models adjust for Big Five personality traits, party identification, age, gender, and income. The Big Five are measured with a 44-item inventory.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We rely on the Big Five Inventory (BFI) consisting of 44 items to measure the five major personality traits [76].",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "All models control for the Big\nFive personality traits, income, age, gender, and party identification.",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The cross-sectional models pool observations across survey waves, use OLS with user-clustered standard errors, and address missing measures with multiple imputation and full-information maximum likelihood.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For the cross-sectional analyses, we use ordinary least squares (OLS) regression with cluster robust standard errors to\naccount for the fact that a single X user can participate across multiple waves.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "This dataset is organized as a Time-Series\nCross-Section (TSCS) dataset using pooled OLS. Not all measures are available in each survey wave and we therefore\nuse multiple imputation and full information maximum likelihood to estimate missing values in these analyses.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The polarization analysis compares users above and below the scale midpoint and separately fits formal interactions. These models adjust for age and gender rather than the full control set to reduce multicollinearity.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Fig 4 displays the associations separately for users above and below the midpoint\nof the leader-directed affective-polarization scale.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "All models control for age and gender but not the full set of controls to reduce problems of multicollinearity. The figure illustrates the interaction term from\neach model from the interaction between the engagement indicators and leader-directed affective polarization.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Random-intercept cross-lagged panel models use behavioral measures of online engagement and hostility to distinguish changes within users over time from stable differences between users.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For the panel analyses, we use Random-Intercept Cross-Lagged Panel Models (RI-CLPM). These models separate\nstable between-person differences from within-person change",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "we use RI-CLPMs with behavioral measures of online political engagement and online\nhostility.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Exploratory negative-binomial models examine counts of posts, accounts followed, and followers, with estimates on the original count scale.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The\nexploratory activity and reach models are an exception: these are negative-binomial count models, and their estimates are\nreported on the original count scale.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "those who are politically hostile (a) post more, (b) have more friends, and (c) have more followers. A “friend” in X’s usage\nis someone I follow, and a “follower” is someone who follows me.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors address multiple testing separately for the primary associations, robustness outcomes, polarization interactions, and exploratory activity and reach models, using Benjamini–Hochberg corrections and reporting Bonferroni-adjusted confidence intervals.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We define four separate families of tests: the primary association models,\nthe robustness outcome models, the affective-polarization interaction models, and the exploratory activity/reach models.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "Within each family we apply a Benjamini–Hochberg false-discovery-rate (FDR) correction and, for the more conservative\nreading requested by the reviewers, also report Bonferroni-adjusted 95% confidence intervals.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Data and scripts for the main analyses are available on OSF, but the raw posts and network files containing identifiers are not public because of re-identification risks.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Data and scripts to reproduce the main analyses can be found on OSF.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "Raw\npost-level X/Twitter data and identifier-bearing\nauxiliary network files cannot be made publicly\navailable because they contain information that\ncould create re-identification risks for survey\nparticipants.",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "Political interest, political knowledge, and online participation show the strongest associations with political hostility. Positive associations are observed after adjusting for broad personality traits, demographics, and party identification.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Online political hostility is positively associated with these indicators, with the\nstrongest associations for political interest, political knowledge, and online participation.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "The associations were obtained after controlling for\nbroad personality traits, income, age, gender, and party identification.",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The associations are generally larger for the specifically political hostility measure than for general sentiment or toxicity. They also persist with alternative classifiers and after accounting for the share of each user’s posts that are political.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The largest associations are generally observed for the\nprimary measure of political hostility rather than for the more general AFINN or Perspective API measures.",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "The robustness analyses are broadly consistent with the main pattern, with significant and sizeable associations across\nall three measures.",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "if anything the results are slightly stronger when controlling for the share of political posts. This suggests that the results are not simply driven by more politically engaged users posting more about politics.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The engagement–hostility link is often stronger among users with more polarized feelings toward leaders, but the clearest evidence is for online and offline participation. Eight of eighteen interactions survive false-discovery-rate correction, with the surviving results concentrated among participation measures.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "for those low on affective polarization only\nfour out of eighteen associations, across our three measures of online hostility, are significant, whereas for those high on\naffective polarization fourteen out of eighteen are significant.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "Formal interaction models (Fig 3) show\nthe clearest support for online and offline participation",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "of the eighteen interaction terms, eight\nremain significant after a Benjamini–Hochberg correction, and these are concentrated among the participatory engagement indicators.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Within individuals, increases in online political engagement precede higher hostility across all three measures. The reverse temporal pattern appears as well, but it is weaker and less consistent.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "In brief, within-person increases in online political engagement\nare followed by higher levels of online political hostility across the three hostility measures. We also observe some evidence of the reverse temporal association, in which hostility is followed by higher political engagement, although this\npattern is weaker and less consistent.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "More politically hostile users post more and have more followers and, to a lesser degree, follow more accounts. Comparing mean hostility with a level 1.5 standard deviations higher, the modeled counts rise from about 420 to 900 posts and from about 220 to 370 followers.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The count-model results indicate that higher political hostility is associated with more posts and with larger numbers\nof followers and, to a lesser extent, friends.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "A change from the mean level of hostility to 1.5 standard deviations above\nthe mean is associated with an increase in the number of posts from around 420 to around 900 and an increase in the\nnumber of followers from around 220 to around 370.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "The results come from American users of a public, text-based, highly political platform and may not carry over to platforms with different audiences, features, moderation, or norms. The Danish X/Twitter evidence is only a tentative indication of broader applicability.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the associations observed here may not generalize directly to platforms with different affordances, audiences, moderation systems, or norms of interaction.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "Some tentative evidence of broader applicability is provided in S1 Appendix, where online political hostility in a sample of\nDanish X/Twitter users is also positively associated with users’ political interest, internal efficacy, and news consumption",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The observational analyses do not establish that engagement causes hostility. The within-person models account for stable differences, but time-varying confounding, measurement error, and reciprocal effects remain possible.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The RI-CLPM analyses reduce concerns about stable time-invariant confounding\nby focusing on within-person change, but they do not rule out time-varying confounding, reciprocal relationships, or measurement error.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "The findings should overall be interpreted as associational rather than causal.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The analyses do not settle how the engagement indicators and their antecedents causally relate to one another. In particular, the results do not imply that more educated users are more hostile apart from their generally higher engagement.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the present findings are mute on the exact causal relationships between the different indicators of political engagement and their antecedents.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "An important caveat in this respect is that the analyses should not be taken to imply\nthat, for example, more educated users are more hostile absent their generally higher levels of political engagement.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The polarization comparisons show conditional associations, not that polarized feelings causally change the effect of engagement on hostility.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We therefore interpret the moderation analyses as descriptive\nevidence about conditional associations rather than as evidence that affective polarization causally moderates the effect\nof engagement on hostility.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The polarization measure concerns feelings about leaders. The study does not show whether the same conditional patterns hold for feelings about parties or their ordinary supporters.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "our measure of affective polarization captures feelings toward party elites rather than\ntoward parties as institutions or ordinary party supporters",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "Future research should examine whether the same\nconditional patterns appear using party-directed or supporter-directed measures.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study concerns hostility grounded in political differences, not nonpolitical toxicity, and its primary measure is not intended to identify the narrower category of hate speech.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We define online political hostility as the expression of degrading, malicious, and\nintimidating online behavior towards individuals or collectives based on political\ndifferences.",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "Accordingly, toxic online exchanges in the aftermath of, say, a\nsports game fall outside the purview of what we examine here.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "we do not wish to confuse this more general measure of online political hostility with the narrower concept of “hate\nspeech” which we are not purporting to measure.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Validation does not eliminate the possibility that the automated score partly measures politically salient conflict vocabulary rather than hostility itself. The authors do not present their measures as exhaustive.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The measure may partly capture\nproximity to politically salient conflict language or hate-related political vocabulary rather than hostility alone.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "We thus do not\nmean to imply that the ways of measuring online hostility presented here are the only ways to measure it.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study does not decide between grievance and personality accounts. In particular, controlling for the Big Five is not a direct test of Dark Triad traits, sadism, or other antagonistic traits that the survey did not measure.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The present study does not seek to adjudicate between these literatures.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "These controls are not intended as a direct test of Dark Triad,\nsadism, or other antagonistic-trait accounts of online hostility. The survey did not include measures of those more specific\ntraits, and we therefore cannot adjudicate those accounts directly.",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The activity and audience counts indicate potential reach, not formal network influence, centrality, or community structure.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "These results suggest greater activity and potential reach, but they\nshould not be interpreted as evidence of formal network influence or centrality.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "we do\nnot draw conclusions about formal network influence, centrality, or community structure.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors retain classical political-behavior theory’s account of the capacities and motivations that support participation, but combine it with revisionist work on the costs of engagement. They extend that revisionist perspective, previously applied online mainly to misinformation sharing, to hostile political expression.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Building on classical theories of political engagement [17,19] and recent work on the darker sides of engagement, we\nfind that online political hostility is positively associated with several indicators of political engagement and its antecedents.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "political engagement to politics on social media and mainly in the context of misinformation sharing [53]. Here, we extend\nand integrate the study of the negative side of political engagement into the study of online politics with a specific focus on\nonline political hostility.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors draw on affective-polarization research to explain why the same political involvement may be expressed more antagonistically when users feel more negatively toward opposing political elites.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Second, we provide suggestive analyses of whether these associations differ by affective polarization [22],\nmeasured as polarized affect towards political leaders.",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "Affective polarization may shape whether such participation is expressed in\nantagonistic terms toward political opponents. Under this logic, the association between engagement and hostility should\nbe stronger among users who evaluate opposing political elites more negatively.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "interlocutor_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Gabriel Almond and Sidney Verba argue that democratic citizenship requires participation tempered by restraint: citizens should be involved, but not excessively so. The paper builds on this qualification by examining whether engagement accompanies hostility online.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "“the democratic citizen is called on to pursue contradictory\ngoals: he must be active, yet passive; involved, yet not too involved; influential, yet deferential.”",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "This potential negative side of political engagement is the key focus of\nour empirical investigation.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
